          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "THE KÄHLER CONDITION dω = 0.\n   Here ω =  1/(yv^2+xv^2+1)^2  dx ∧ dy, a 2-form on a 2-dimensional space.\n   There are no 3-forms in 2 dimensions, so dω = 0 automatically:\n   EVERY Hermitian metric on a Riemann surface is Kähler.  The\n   condition only starts to bite in complex dimension 2 and above.\nAREA.  ∫∫ dx dy/(1+x²+y²)² in polar coordinates:\n   2π ∫₀^∞ ρ dρ/(1+ρ²)² =  %pi\n   A sphere of radius 1/2 has area 4πR² =  %pi   ✓\n   (The plane covers the sphere minus one point, which has measure\n   zero, so the integral is the whole area.)\nGAUSS–BONNET (3.08, 5.06):\n   ∫K dA / 2π =  2  = χ(S²) ✓\n   K = 4 everywhere and the area is π; the product is forced to be\n   4π by topology, whatever the geometry.\nAND THE CHERN CLASS.  5.06 found that a U(1) bundle over S² has\n∫F/2π = n ∈ ℤ, with n = 1 the Hopf bundle.  Here\n   ∫ω/π =  1\n   so ω/π is the generator of H²(ℂP¹;ℤ), which is c₁ of the\n   hyperplane bundle — the n = 1 monopole of 5.06, met again as a\n   Kähler form.  The symplectic form and the curvature of a line\n   bundle are the same 2-form.\nBEYOND COMPLEX DIMENSION 1.\n   Kähler ⟹ symplectic, and Kähler ⟹ complex, but neither converse\n   holds.  The Kodaira–Thurston manifold is compact symplectic and\n   admits no Kähler metric at all: its first Betti number is odd,\n   and Hodge theory forces b₁ to be EVEN on a compact Kähler\n   manifold.  A purely topological obstruction to a geometric\n   structure — the same flavour of argument as 4.07's Gauss–Bonnet\n   constraint on which surfaces admit which curvature.\n   ℂPⁿ with the Fubini–Study metric is Kähler for every n, is\n   SU(n+1)/S(U(n)×U(1)) as 5.03 computed, and is a symmetric space\n   of rank 1 in the sense of 5.04 — so it sits at the intersection\n   of every thread in Stage 5."
          ]
        }
      ]
