    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Summary\n\n$$\\omega(X,Y) = g(JX,Y), \\qquad J^2 = -\\mathrm{id}, \\qquad \\omega = i\\partial\\bar\\partial K$$\n\n**Three structures, two free.** With $J$ multiplication by $i$ written real-linearly on $\\mathbb{R}^4 = \\mathbb{C}^2$: $J^2 = -I$, $J^TgJ = g$, and $\\Omega = J^Tg$ came out antisymmetric with $\\det\\Omega = 1$ — a symplectic form of exactly 6.03's shape. Recovering $J$ from $(g,\\Omega)$ and $g$ from $(J,\\Omega)$ both returned the originals. **Any two determine the third**, and a Kähler manifold is one object that Riemannian geometry, symplectic geometry and complex analysis are all looking at.\n\n**Even dimension, three times over.** $J^2 = -I_n$ forces $(\\det J)^2 = (-1)^n$, so $n$ must be even. That is the same parity argument that made symplectic manifolds even-dimensional in 6.03 and restricted the Euler class to even rank in 5.06 — one fact about antisymmetry, showing up in three places.\n\n**One function is the whole metric.** From $K = \\log(1+|z|^2)$, differentiating once in $z$ and once in $\\bar z$ gives\n\n$$g_{z\\bar z} = \\frac{1}{(1+|z|^2)^2}$$\n\ndirectly. A general metric in real dimension 4 needs 10 independent components; a Kähler metric needs one scalar.\n\n**The Fubini–Study metric is the sphere of radius $\\tfrac12$.** In real coordinates $ds^2 = (dx^2+dy^2)/(1+x^2+y^2)^2$, and the curvature came out $K = 4$ by two independent routes — the conformal formula $-e^{-2\\varphi}\\Delta\\varphi$, and the full Christoffel-and-Riemann machinery of 4.04. Since $K = 1/R^2$, that is $R = \\tfrac12$.\n\nAnd it really *is* that sphere. Inverse stereographic projection of the unit sphere (2.05's map) has first fundamental form $E = G = 4/(1+r^2)^2$, $F = 0$ — conformal, as 2.05 required — and $4\\cdot g_{\\text{FS}} - E = 0$ identically. **Stereographic projection and the Kähler potential $\\log(1+|z|^2)$ are the same construction**, reached from circle geometry in Stage 2 and from complex analysis here.\n\n**Everything closes.**\n\n| computed | value | agrees with |\n|---|---|---|\n| area of $\\mathbb{CP}^1$ | $\\pi$ | $4\\pi R^2$ at $R = \\tfrac12$ |\n| $\\int K\\,dA/2\\pi$ | $2$ | $\\chi(S^2)$, 3.08 and 5.06 |\n| $\\int \\omega/\\pi$ | $1$ | $c_1$ of the Hopf bundle, 5.06's $n = 1$ |\n\nThat last row is worth pausing on: 5.06 found $\\int F/2\\pi = n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ for a $U(1)$ bundle over $S^2$, with $n=1$ the Hopf bundle. Here the *symplectic form* of a Kähler metric is that same generator. **The symplectic form and the curvature of a line bundle are the same 2-form.**\n\n**In complex dimension 1 the Kähler condition is free** — $d\\omega = 0$ automatically, since there are no 3-forms on a surface. So every Hermitian metric on a Riemann surface is Kähler, and the condition only starts to bite from complex dimension 2.\n\n**But it is a real condition.** The Kodaira–Thurston manifold is compact symplectic and admits no Kähler metric: its $b_1$ is odd, and Hodge theory forces $b_1$ even on a compact Kähler manifold. A purely topological obstruction to a geometric structure — the same shape of argument as 4.07's Gauss–Bonnet constraint on which surfaces carry which curvature.\n\n**Integrability is not automatic above dimension 2.** Every almost complex structure on a surface is integrable (the Nijenhuis tensor has nowhere to live), which is why every oriented Riemannian surface is a Riemann surface. $S^2$ and $S^6$ are the only spheres carrying even an almost complex structure, and whether $S^6$ carries an integrable one remains open.\n\n## Next\n\n**6.05 — Information Geometry** finds a Riemannian metric in an unexpected place: the space of probability distributions. The Fisher information is a metric, the exponential family is a flat geometry, and Kullback–Leibler divergence is its squared distance to second order.\n\n## References\n\n- Griffiths, P. and Harris, J. (1978) *Principles of Algebraic Geometry*. New York: Wiley. Chapter 0 §7 for Kähler manifolds and the Fubini–Study metric, computed much as above but in full generality.\n- Huybrechts, D. (2005) *Complex Geometry: An Introduction*. Berlin: Springer. Chapters 1–3. The most approachable modern account of the $g$–$\\omega$–$J$ triple and of when the Kähler condition can be met.\n- Kobayashi, S. and Nomizu, K. (1969) *Foundations of Differential Geometry*, vol. II. New York: Wiley. Chapter IX, for complex and Hermitian structures in the same language as 5.05's bundles.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. [doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9). Chapter 3 problems construct the Fubini–Study metric as a homogeneous metric on $\\mathbb{CP}^n$, complementing the potential-based route here.\n- Newlander, A. and Nirenberg, L. (1957) 'Complex analytic coordinates in almost complex manifolds', *Annals of Mathematics*, 65(3), pp. 391–404. The integrability theorem quoted above.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell was executed. Two things are quoted rather than computed: the Newlander–Nirenberg theorem, and the Hodge-theoretic constraint that $b_1$ is even on a compact Kähler manifold. The Fubini–Study curvature is normalisation-dependent — with $ds^2 = |dz|^2/(1+|z|^2)^2$ as used here it is 4; texts that write $ds^2 = 2|dz|^2/(1+|z|^2)^2$ get 2.*",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}